\subsection*{3. Desugaring and Dunders}

Python's \texttt{for x in c} loop is sugar for calling \texttt{iter(c)}
once, then \texttt{next()} repeatedly until \texttt{StopIteration}. A
generator is a specialized iterator; a class can be its own iterator by
defining \texttt{\_\_iter\_\_} and \texttt{\_\_next\_\_}.

\subsubsection*{3.1 Hand-written Countdown Class}

A class that models a countdown can implement the iterator protocol
directly:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \texttt{\_\_iter\_\_} returns \texttt{self} (or a fresh
        iterator instance) so the class is iterable.
  \item \texttt{\_\_next\_\_} returns the next value or raises
        \texttt{StopIteration}.
\end{itemize}

If \texttt{\_\_iter\_\_} returns \texttt{self}, the class is one-shot
like a generator: consuming it a second time gives an empty pass. If
\texttt{\_\_iter\_\_} returns a fresh iterator each call, the class is
re-iterable.
